WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.270 --> 00:00:01.970
- I hear that like only hot people

00:00:01.970 --> 00:00:03.597
and cool people hear yanny so.

00:00:03.597 --> 00:00:04.520
- [Woman] Shut up.

00:00:04.520 --> 00:00:06.253
- If you think it's yanny,

00:00:06.740 --> 00:00:09.475
I wish to disconnect with you on a personal level.

00:00:09.475 --> 00:00:10.308
(dramatic music)

00:00:10.308 --> 00:00:13.259
(robot saying yanny or laurel)

00:00:13.270 --> 00:00:15.420
- So people have been freaking out

00:00:15.440 --> 00:00:17.820
about this yanny versus laurel debate.

00:00:17.820 --> 00:00:20.953
- There is no way that this could be yanny.

00:00:20.953 --> 00:00:24.290
- This is not saying laurel, this is only saying yanny.

00:00:24.290 --> 00:00:26.713
- It just says laurel a bunch of times.

00:00:26.755 --> 00:00:29.300
- It's just, it's bananas, but I only hear yanny.

00:00:29.300 --> 00:00:31.713
- And we're seeing this story everywhere.

00:00:31.810 --> 00:00:33.919
The New York Times wrote this thing up.

00:00:33.919 --> 00:00:37.963
Even musician Yanni commented on this.

00:00:38.550 --> 00:00:40.790
Some people have really been enjoying this debate

00:00:40.790 --> 00:00:44.370
while others are completely over it, including myself so

00:00:44.370 --> 00:00:46.470
I wanna figure it out once and for all,

00:00:46.470 --> 00:00:48.430
is it yanny, is it laurel

00:00:48.710 --> 00:00:51.623
and how is this phenomenon even happening?

00:00:51.710 --> 00:00:53.970
So to get more of a back story on this,

00:00:53.970 --> 00:00:56.380
I decided that I'm just gonna reach out to the source

00:00:56.380 --> 00:00:59.230
and contact the original Redditor

00:00:59.330 --> 00:01:01.213
that posted the audio clip.

00:01:01.260 --> 00:01:03.520
So I reached out to this guy named Roland

00:01:03.680 --> 00:01:05.590
and he actually was pretty quick to respond.

00:01:05.590 --> 00:01:08.690
He said that he came across the words on a website

00:01:08.690 --> 00:01:12.113
when he working on a project a long time ago.

00:01:12.300 --> 00:01:13.963
I remember my sister saying,

00:01:13.980 --> 00:01:16.787
'Why do you keep playing yanny.'

00:01:17.060 --> 00:01:18.340
And then my sister's friend

00:01:18.340 --> 00:01:20.490
who was doing homework with my sister said,

00:01:20.632 --> 00:01:22.587
'That definitely says laurel.'

00:01:22.620 --> 00:01:24.463
I was very confused after that.

00:01:24.610 --> 00:01:28.023
I don't remember the name of the website unfortunately.

00:01:28.210 --> 00:01:30.103
After doing a little bit of digging,

00:01:30.510 --> 00:01:33.030
I was able to find the website,

00:01:33.030 --> 00:01:37.435
he actually found this audio clip on vocabulary.com,

00:01:37.435 --> 00:01:38.466
(robot saying yanny or laurel)

00:01:38.520 --> 00:01:41.920
which is incredible because it solves the problem

00:01:41.920 --> 00:01:44.830
of what word is being said.

00:01:44.830 --> 00:01:48.380
We can definitely say the word is laurel,

00:01:48.380 --> 00:01:52.713
but we can't argue that it sounds like yanny.

00:01:52.830 --> 00:01:54.780
Sound is how we perceive things

00:01:55.350 --> 00:01:57.820
and I wanna figure out why that is

00:01:57.820 --> 00:02:00.660
so to get to the bottom of this

00:02:01.170 --> 00:02:03.020
I think I need the help of an expert.

00:02:03.130 --> 00:02:04.410
There are a few theories that I had heard

00:02:04.410 --> 00:02:05.770
that I'd love to hear your thoughts on.

00:02:05.770 --> 00:02:10.750
Like one said the age of your ears

00:02:10.770 --> 00:02:12.493
will change how you hear it,

00:02:12.510 --> 00:02:13.860
is there any truth to that?

00:02:27.590 --> 00:02:30.810
Another theory that I had heard was

00:02:31.430 --> 00:02:34.040
this says more about the speakers

00:02:34.090 --> 00:02:36.280
you're listening to the audio from

00:02:36.430 --> 00:02:39.303
than your ears or your perception.

00:02:51.858 --> 00:02:54.083
If you were to play with the frequency

00:02:54.090 --> 00:02:56.950
and move it up or down, you would,

00:02:56.950 --> 00:02:59.410
like the differences would become more transparent

00:02:59.410 --> 00:03:00.910
to the audience, is that true?

00:03:07.110 --> 00:03:09.250
And then if we moved it up we would then see

00:03:09.250 --> 00:03:10.703
or hear the laurel

00:03:10.703 --> 00:03:13.640
- [Mario] It's likely to be laurel, would be my guess, yes.

00:03:13.640 --> 00:03:16.670
- So I decided to put this theory to the test.

00:03:16.670 --> 00:03:20.330
I put the audio through one of my editing programs

00:03:20.390 --> 00:03:22.517
and started playing with the pitch.

00:03:22.517 --> 00:03:23.623
(robot saying yanny or laurel)

00:03:23.660 --> 00:03:26.143
Now I was already hearing laurel,

00:03:26.380 --> 00:03:29.457
but as I started going lower and lower in pitch,

00:03:29.457 --> 00:03:30.290
- [Robot] Yanny.

00:03:30.290 --> 00:03:34.319
- [Host] I started to hear yanny clearer and clearer.

00:03:34.319 --> 00:03:35.320
- [Robot] Yanny.

00:03:35.320 --> 00:03:37.420
- But maybe you were already hearing yanny

00:03:37.840 --> 00:03:39.810
so when you go in the opposite direction,

00:03:39.810 --> 00:03:41.210
I'm sure you'll agree...

00:03:41.210 --> 00:03:42.043
- [Robot] Laurel.

00:03:42.043 --> 00:03:43.057
- [Host] You start hearing...

00:03:43.057 --> 00:03:43.890
- [Robot] Laurel.

00:03:43.890 --> 00:03:44.976
- [Host] Laurel.

00:03:45.020 --> 00:03:45.853
- [Robot] Laurel.

00:03:45.853 --> 00:03:46.910
- So now what does this mean?

00:03:47.290 --> 00:03:49.260
Well it means we can stop talking about it.

00:03:49.260 --> 00:03:52.927
(dramatic orchestral music)

